      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2010 International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Previous studies have demonstrated considerable variation in the antiplatelet effect of aspirin."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To investigate the impact of platelet turnover on the antiplatelet effect of aspirin in patients with stable coronary artery disease (CAD) and to identify determinants of platelet turnover."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Platelet turnover was evaluated by measurements of immature platelets and thrombopoietin in 177 stable CAD patients on aspirin monotherapy, including 85 type 2 diabetics and 92 non-diabetics. Whole blood platelet aggregation was determined using the VerifyNow(®) Aspirin test and multiple electrode aggregometry (MEA, Multiplate(®) ) induced by arachidonic acid (AA) (1.0 mm), adenosine diphosphate (ADP) (10 μm) and collagen (1.0 μg mL(-1) )."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Immature platelet levels significantly correlated with MEA (r = 0.31-0.36, P-values < 0.0001) and the platelet activation marker sP-selectin (r = 0.19, P = 0.014). Contrary to the VerifyNow(®) test, MEA significantly correlated with variations in platelet count (r = 0.45-0.68, P-values < 0.0001). Among patients with residual platelet reactivity according to AA, there were significantly more diabetics (61% vs. 41%, P = 0.027) and higher levels of sP-selectin (77.7 ± 29 vs. 70.2 ± 25 ng mL(-1) , P = 0.070) and serum thromboxane B(2) (0.81 [0.46; 1.70] vs. 0.56 [0.31; 1.12] ng mL(-1) , P = 0.034). In a multivariate regression analysis, immature platelet levels were determined by thrombopoietin levels (P < 0.001), smoking (P = 0.020) and type 2 diabetes (P = 0.042)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The antiplatelet effect of aspirin was reduced in CAD patients with an increased platelet turnover. Once-daily dosing of aspirin might not suffice to adequately inhibit platelet aggregation in patients with an increased platelet turnover."
          }
        ]
      },
